    "body": "### What does this PR do?\n\nWhen an optional dependency is missing (for example `qwen_vl_utils` for multimodal data), `_process_multi_modal_info` raises `ModuleNotFoundError` inside `doc2len` for every sample. The catch-all `except Exception` in `maybe_filter_out_long_prompts`, added in #3814 to drop malformed rows, swallows it and returns `max_prompt_length + 1`, so the filter silently drops the whole dataset. Training then fails much later with an unrelated error:\n\n```\nValueError: num_samples should be a positive integer value, but got num_samples=0\n```\n\nA missing package is an environment problem, not malformed data, so this PR re-raises `ImportError` (which covers `ModuleNotFoundError`) in both `doc2len` paths. Malformed rows are still dropped as before.\n\nRelated: #6888 moves these two functions to module level (`_filter_prompt_length_with_processor` / `_filter_prompt_length_with_tokenizer`). If it lands first, the same two lines apply there and I'm happy to rebase.\n\n### Checklist Before Starting\n\n- [x] Search for similar PRs. Paste at least one query link here: https://github.com/verl-project/verl/pulls?q=is%3Apr+doc2len\n- [x] Format the PR title as `[{modules}] {type}: {description}` (This will be checked by the CI)\n\n### Test\n\nAdded `test_maybe_filter_out_long_prompts_reraises_import_error` to `tests/utils/dataset/test_rl_dataset_on_cpu.py`. It patches `_process_multi_modal_info` to raise `ModuleNotFoundError` and asserts the filter raises `ImportError` instead of returning an empty dataset.\n\n- Before the fix: `Failed: DID NOT RAISE ImportError` (captured output shows `filter dataset len: 0`)\n- After the fix: passes, and all 11 tests in the file pass locally\n\nFound while running the CPU dataset tests locally without `qwen_vl_utils` installed: `test_image_rl_data` failed with the `num_samples=0` error above.\n\n### API and Usage Example\n\nNo API change.\n\n### Design & Code Changes\n\n- `verl/utils/dataset/rl_dataset.py`: add `except ImportError: raise` before `except Exception` in both `doc2len` closures (processor and tokenizer paths).\n- `tests/utils/dataset/test_rl_dataset_on_cpu.py`: add the regression test above.\n\n### Checklist Before Submitting\n\n- [x] Read the [Contribute Guide](https://github.com/verl-project/verl/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md).\n- [x] Apply [pre-commit checks](https://github.com/verl-project/verl/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#code-linting-and-formatting) (run on the changed files; all hooks pass).\n- [ ] Add / Update [the documentation](https://github.com/verl-project/verl/tree/main/docs). Not needed: no user-facing behaviour change beyond a clearer error.\n- [x] Add unit or end-to-end test(s) to [the CI workflow](https://github.com/verl-project/verl/tree/main/.github/workflows) to cover all the code. The new test is in an `*_on_cpu.py` file, so `cpu_unit_tests.yml` picks it up.\n- [ ] Once your PR is ready for CI, send a message in the `ci-request` channel.\n- [ ] If your PR is related to the `recipe` submodule, please also update the reference to the submodule commit. Not applicable.\n",
